 * store_buyer_pay.c, and test_store_buyer.c's "bytes on disk" check reads the
 * collected file back at the resolved path -- so a wrong resolution is a red
 * assertion. The other two production callers (the hodl snapshot page and the
 * shop native probe) are not driven by any registered group. */
AGENT_IMPACT_RULE("lib/platform/src/private_destination.c", "store_buyer make_lint_gates")

/* The read-mapping header, whose implementation already has its own rule.
 * Three groups execute the surface it declares: read_mapping_positioned calls
 * platform_read_mapping_open_positioned directly and asserts the mapped bytes
 * against a positioned read; node_db_catchup_service is the group named by
 * the implementation's own rule; and block_index_loader reaches it twice, via
 * load_block_index_flat in block_index_loader.c and via
 * block_index_flat_header_at, whose point-read assertion depends on the map
 * being correct. Deliberately not the whole list of files that include this
 * header -- being a compile dependency of a group is not being executed by
 * it, and these three assert on mapped bytes. */
AGENT_IMPACT_RULE("lib/platform/include/platform/read_mapping.h", "read_mapping_positioned node_db_catchup_service block_index_loader make_lint_gates")

/* Deferred HTTPS activation, split out of https_server.c. NO REGISTERED GROUP
 * EXECUTES IT, so the lint tier alone is the honest answer. Checked, not
 * assumed: https_deferred_set has one caller, boot_frontend_services.c, which
 * no test invokes; https_deferred_check has two, the reached_peer_tip arm of
 * process_block_msg and the periodic AT_TIP commit in sync_monitor.c --
 * test_msg_handlers.c does call process_block_msg, but all five of its cases
 * are unrequested-block scoring and reducer-queueing cases that never make a
 * valid block the new tip, and sync_monitor_evaluate_tip_state appears in no
 * test at all; and https_deferred_pending is read only by two diagnostics
 * dumpers, whose dump function pointers no test invokes -- test_rpc.c's
 * dumpstate catalog case reads the entry METADATA and asserts e->fn != NULL
 * without ever calling it. Closing this needs a case that calls
 * https_deferred_set() and asserts https_deferred_pending() flips true, then
 * drives sync_monitor_evaluate_tip_state() to its AT_TIP commit and asserts
 * it flips back false. */
AGENT_IMPACT_RULE("lib/net/src/https_deferred.c", "make_lint_gates")

/* Five fail-closed Windows boundary units the merge split out of their
 * POSIX siblings. Every one is a single #if defined(_WIN32) block, so on this
 * host each compiles to an EMPTY object and no line of it can execute. The
 * lint tier alone is therefore the honest answer, and the sibling's group is
 * deliberately NOT named: it would grade the POSIX file, not this one.
 * check-windows-acceptance cross-links four of them (build_fabric_worker,
 * consensus_state_publication_cas, zcode_benchmark_executor and the snapshot
 * activate arm each appear in a windows_acceptance.mk SOURCES row) and
 * check-windows-platform-seam mingw-syntax-checks private_acl_internal.c,
 * whose scan root is the lib/platform src directory -- but a cross-compile is
 * not an execution. Only that second gate is in LINT_FAST_GATES, so the push
 * gate sees just the one. Closing this needs a Windows host or Wine actually
 * running the acceptance binaries and asserting the refusal strings. */
AGENT_IMPACT_RULE("app/services/src/build_fabric_worker_windows.c|app/services/src/consensus_state_publication_cas_windows.c|app/services/src/zcode_benchmark_executor_windows.c|config/src/consensus_state_snapshot_install_activate_windows.c|lib/platform/src/private_acl_internal.c", "make_lint_gates")

/* The dev-loop watcher family and its two neighbours: the directory-change
 * watcher, the watcher lease and launch handoff, the watcher record codec,
 * the watcher store, the process lifecycle wrapper, the directory
 * transaction, and platform_state_root, each with the public header the merge
 * added beside it. NO PRODUCTION CALLER OF ANY OF THEM EXECUTES ON THIS
 * HOST, and unlike the stub rule above that is not because the code is
 * Windows-only -- these files have real POSIX arms. It is because every
 * CALLER is Windows-only. Checked call site by call site with the
 * preprocessor state tracked: each use of platform_state_root,
 * platform_watcher_*, platform_process_start_hidden and the
 * platform_directory_transaction and
 * platform_directory_child families in tools/command and tools/dev sits
 * inside an #if defined(_WIN32) arm -- including the two that DO reach the
 * test binary through TEST_DEV_EXECUTOR_SRCS, rr_copy_publish in
 * devloop_restart_build.c and store_open in dev_failure_store.c, both of
 * which take a plain POSIX path here. The only unguarded consumers are in
 * devloop_cli.c and devloop_watch.c, which are DEV_ONLY_SRCS: linked into the
 * dev binary only, never the test binary, and zcl_devloop_cli_main is named
 * nowhere under lib/test/src.
 *
 * Two of the family are now covered by execution rather than by a caller. The
 * two probe programs that used to sit dead under lib/platform/tests were
 * rehomed into lib/test/src as registered groups (see their rule above), and
 * each drives exactly one member of this family directly:
 *
 *   directory_watcher.c  -- the directory_watcher group opens a scratch root
 *     through platform_directory_watcher_open, takes the STOPPED verdict from
 *     the stop predicate, and asserts CHANGED for a create and again for a
 *     rename+unlink. That is the inotify arm of this file end to end.
 *   watcher_record.c     -- the watcher_record group serializes, re-encodes
 *     byte-identically, parses, refuses six malformed encodings, and walks
 *     every member of the binding for a mismatch. The module is pure and has
 *     no _WIN32 arm, so this is the whole file.
 *
 * Named by execution only: neither group so much as links a symbol from
 * watcher_lease.c, watcher_store.c, process_lifecycle.c,
 * directory_transaction.c or state_root.c, so those keep the lint tier
 * alone. What reads THEM is check-windows-platform-seam, an -fsyntax-only
 * mingw pass over the lib/platform src directory that IS in
 * LINT_FAST_GATES, plus their
 * cross-compiled rows in lib/platform/tests/windows_acceptance.mk -- a
 * compile, not an execution. Closing the rest needs registered lib/test/src
 * groups driving those POSIX arms directly -- take and release the store
 * lock, acquire and drop a lease -- the way the two above now do. */
AGENT_IMPACT_RULE("lib/platform/src/directory_watcher.c|lib/platform/include/platform/directory_watcher.h", "directory_watcher make_lint_gates")
AGENT_IMPACT_RULE("lib/platform/src/watcher_record.c|lib/platform/include/platform/watcher_record.h", "watcher_record make_lint_gates")
AGENT_IMPACT_RULE("lib/platform/src/watcher_lease.c|lib/platform/include/platform/watcher_lease.h|lib/platform/src/watcher_store.c|lib/platform/include/platform/watcher_store.h|lib/platform/src/process_lifecycle.c|lib/platform/include/platform/process_lifecycle.h|lib/platform/src/directory_transaction.c|lib/platform/src/state_root.c|lib/platform/include/platform/state_root.h", "make_lint_gates")

/* ══ Five inputs the merge left unmapped ═════════════════════════════════
 *
 * Each of the five below failed the fail-closed refusal on its own. Three are
 * headers or a makefile that the sibling rules above could never match --
 * those rules name .c files -- and the other two are a platform TU and a test
 * source that had no rule at all. Every verdict here was re-derived from the
 * symbols and the preprocessor state, not inherited from a neighbour. */

/* The public header of the retained, owner-verified directory handle. Its .c
 * is carried by the watcher-family rule above, which paired every other
 * member of that family with the header the merge added beside it and missed
 * this one. Re-checked rather than inherited, and the verdict is the same:
 * NOTHING under lib/test/src names a platform_directory_transaction_,
 * platform_directory_child_ or platform_directory_lock_ symbol, and every one
 * of the twelve includers in tools/command and tools/dev opens its use with
 * "#if defined(_WIN32)" -- checked line by line, the first use in each file
 * sits inside that arm. So no line of the POSIX arm executes on this host and
 * the lint tier alone is honest. What observes it is
 * check-windows-platform-seam, whose mingw -fsyntax-only pass over
 * lib/platform/src compiles directory_transaction.c and therefore this
 * header, plus the directory_transaction and watcher_store rows of the
 * acceptance catalog -- a compile, not an execution. Closing this needs a
 * registered lib/test/src group that opens a scratch directory through
 * platform_directory_transaction_open and drives the child
 * create/write/replace/unlink path directly. */
AGENT_IMPACT_RULE("lib/platform/include/platform/directory_transaction.h", "make_lint_gates")

/* The internal header of the Windows owner+SYSTEM ACL helper. Its .c sits in
 * the five-Windows-boundary-units rule above, which names .c files only, so
 * the header failed closed by itself. Every declaration it makes is inside a
 * single "#if defined(_WIN32)" block, so on this host the header expands to
 * nothing and no function it declares can be called by anything. Lint tier
 * alone, for the same reason the .c has it: check-windows-platform-seam
 * mingw-syntax-checks the four TUs that include it -- private_acl_internal.c,
 * private_directory.c, watcher_lease.c and directory_transaction.c -- and the
 * watcher_lease and watcher_store catalog rows cross-link the .c. Closing
 * this needs a Windows host or Wine running those acceptance binaries. */
AGENT_IMPACT_RULE("lib/platform/src/private_acl_internal.h", "make_lint_gates")

/* The retained cross-process lock file. Unlike the rest of the watcher family
 * this one IS executed on POSIX, and load-bearingly, so it gets a group and
 * not the lint tier. tools/dev/dev_activation.c's dev_acquire_lock() calls
 * platform_process_lock_try_acquire(create=true) with NO _WIN32 guard, and
 * dev_activation.c is in DEVLOOP_SRCS rather than DEV_ONLY_SRCS, so it links
 * into the test binary. lib/test/src/test_dev_activation.c then drives the
 * module both ways: every non-refusing dev_activation_run() case has to get a
 * true back from the acquire before it can reach dev_run_locked() at all, and
 * the case "a held activation lock yields E_LOCK_BUSY" takes an flock on
 * <gen_root>/activation.lock in the test process first, then asserts
 * dev_activation_run() returns DEV_ACTIVATION_E_LOCK_BUSY and that the fake
 * ops saw zero stop calls -- so the refusal arm is asserted, not merely
 * compiled. dev_release_lock() exercises release and the `held` bookkeeping
 * on the way out. Deliberately NOT counting tools/dev/dev_failure_store.c:
 * its store_lock() reaches this module only inside "#if defined(_WIN32)" and
 * its POSIX arm uses openat+flock directly. The header is named beside the
 * .c because it declares exactly these three functions and nothing else. */
AGENT_IMPACT_RULE("lib/platform/src/process_lock.c|lib/platform/include/platform/process_lock.h", "dev_activation make_lint_gates")

/* The Windows acceptance catalog itself. It is a makefile, not C, and is
 * mapped the way the other non-C gate inputs in this file are -- the
 * tools/lint check scripts and their baseline goldens. NO TEST READS IT: three
 * files under lib/test/src mention the catalog in a PROSE COMMENT and none
 * opens it, nothing under lib/test/src names check-windows-acceptance, and
 * the catalog declares no ZCL_TEST_GROUP row. What reads it is
 * tools/lint/check_windows_acceptance.sh, which mingw-cross-links every
 * program the catalog declares and reconciles the two directions, and the
 * top-level Makefile, which includes the catalog so the
 * ZCL_WINDOWS_ACCEPTANCE_ variables reach that gate. check-windows-acceptance
 * is in LINT_GATES but NOT in LINT_FAST_GATES, so only a full `make lint`
 * observes an edit here -- weaker than a group, and said plainly rather than
 * dressed up. The lint tier alone is the honest answer. */
AGENT_IMPACT_RULE("lib/platform/tests/windows_acceptance.mk", "make_lint_gates")
/* This headless program is compiled only by the Windows acceptance catalog;
 * the lint tier reconciles and cross-links that catalog fail-closed. */
AGENT_IMPACT_RULE("lib/platform/tests/datadir_privacy_windows_acceptance.c", "make_lint_gates")

/* The zcode sovereignty-policy test's own source. zcode_sovereignty_policy is
 * a registered group -- tools/dev/test_group_catalog.def carries the
 * ZCL_TEST_GROUP row and tools/dev/test-group-list.sh emits it -- and this
 * file defines the int test_zcode_sovereignty_policy(void) entry that row
 * names. A test source's own group is the only group that executes it, so
 * that group plus the lint tier is the whole mapping; the implementation it
 * drives, lib/vcs/src/zcode_sovereignty_policy.c, is deliberately left out so
 * that a change to the implementation still trips the fail-closed refusal and
 * earns its own reasoning. */
AGENT_IMPACT_RULE("lib/test/src/test_zcode_sovereignty_policy.c", "zcode_sovereignty_policy make_lint_gates")
/* The module-order declaration is the ONE place the lib module set and link
 * rank are written: the Makefile derives LIB_MODULES from it and codeindex
 * pastes it into k_lib_modules[], so its proofs are the lint-gate selftests
 * (which pin check_lib_module_order's rank/set enforcement) and the codeindex
 * group (whose case 6 compares the declaration to the lib/ tree). */
AGENT_IMPACT_RULE("config/lib_module_order.def", "make_lint_gates codeindex")
/* The script group's own test source: editing it is proven by running it,
 * with the lint-gate selftests for the shared harness it touches. */
AGENT_IMPACT_RULE("lib/test/src/test_script.c", "script make_lint_gates")
